    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 2, 1, $vp$What This Module Covers$vp$, $vp$overview$vp$, $vp$The NEC is the rulebook. It's also where most apprentices get stuck on the exam — not because the rules are unfair, but because the code book itself is a strange beast and you have to know how to navigate it before you can answer questions out of it.

This module covers the introduction to the code (Article 90), the definitions you have to know cold (Article 100), the general installation requirements that apply everywhere (Article 110), and the mechanics of finding what you need fast. By the end you should be able to flip to a relevant section in under 30 seconds, which on a timed open-book exam is the difference between passing and not.

**By the end of this module, you'll be able to:**

- Explain the scope and arrangement of the NEC and what each chapter covers
- Distinguish between mandatory rules, permissive rules, and explanatory material
- Identify the Article 100 definitions that matter most for the apprentice exam
- Apply general installation requirements from Article 110 (working space, identification, terminations)
- Use the index and table of contents efficiently during an open-book exam
- Recognize when local amendments or AHJ judgment override the code text

Bring your code book to this one. Reading about the NEC isn't the same as flipping through it. If you can, sit down with your physical or digital copy as you work through the sections.$vp$, 2, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$Concept-only overview; no code citation needed.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 2, 2, $vp$How the Code Is Organized$vp$, $vp$concept$vp$, $vp$The NEC is published by the National Fire Protection Association (NFPA) as **NFPA 70**. It's revised on a three-year cycle. The 2026 edition is the most recent at the time of this writing, though many jurisdictions are still operating under the 2023 or even 2020 edition — the AHJ (authority having jurisdiction) decides which edition is in force locally.

## The structure

The code is divided into an **introduction (Article 90)** and **nine chapters**, plus annexes. NEC 90.3 lays out the arrangement:

- **Chapter 1 — General.** Definitions (Article 100), general requirements (Article 110).
- **Chapter 2 — Wiring and Protection.** Branch circuits, feeders, services, overcurrent protection, grounding.
- **Chapter 3 — Wiring Methods and Materials.** Cable types, raceways, boxes, conductors.
- **Chapter 4 — Equipment for General Use.** Cords, switches, receptacles, appliances, motors.
- **Chapter 5 — Special Occupancies.** Hazardous locations, healthcare, places of assembly, etc.
- **Chapter 6 — Special Equipment.** Signs, cranes, elevators, swimming pools, solar PV, etc.
- **Chapter 7 — Special Conditions.** Emergency systems, standby, interconnected sources.
- **Chapter 8 — Communications Systems.** Stand-alone — Chapters 1-7 only apply if Chapter 8 specifically references them.
- **Chapter 9 — Tables.** Conduit fill, voltage drop, conductor properties.

**Chapters 1 through 4 apply generally.** Chapters 5 through 7 supplement or modify Chapters 1 through 4 — meaning a rule in Chapter 5 about a hazardous location overrides the general rule in Chapter 3 if they conflict.

**Chapter 8 is special.** It stands alone. General code rules don't apply to communications systems unless Chapter 8 specifically calls them out.

## Mandatory vs permissive vs explanatory

NEC 90.5 splits code text into three categories:

- **Mandatory rules** use *shall* or *shall not*. These are enforceable. "Branch-circuit conductors shall have an ampacity not less than..." — that's a requirement.
- **Permissive rules** use *shall be permitted* or *shall not be required*. These describe options or alternatives. "A grounding electrode conductor *shall be permitted* to be solid or stranded..." — you can do either.
- **Explanatory material** appears as Informational Notes, exceptions, and informative annexes. **Informational Notes are NOT enforceable** — they explain or reference, but you can't get cited for ignoring one. Exceptions, on the other hand, are enforceable: they modify the rule they follow.

Confusing an Informational Note with a mandatory rule is one of the most common apprentice mistakes. The 3% / 5% voltage-drop guidance, for example, lives in an Informational Note — it's a strong recommendation, not law.

## Article numbering

Code references follow a predictable pattern: **chapter.article.section(subsection)**. So `250.66(A)(1)` means Article 250 (grounding), section 66 (grounding electrode conductor for AC systems), subsection (A), sub-sub (1). Once you internalize the pattern, you can find anything by structure alone — no index needed.$vp$, 5, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 90.3 (Code Arrangement), 90.5 (Mandatory/Permissive/Explanatory). Chapter structure verified in PDF. Three-year cycle is standard NFPA practice. NEC 2026 verified as current edition. Voltage-drop Informational Note location verified earlier.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 2, 3, $vp$Articles 90, 100, and 110$vp$, $vp$code_reference$vp$, $vp$Three articles set up everything else. Know what's in each.

## Article 90 — Introduction

Article 90 tells you what the code does and doesn't cover.

**NEC 90.1 — Scope.** The code covers use and application, arrangement, and enforcement.

**NEC 90.2 — Scope and Application of Document.** The code applies to installation of electrical conductors, equipment, and raceways for premises wiring. It does NOT cover utility-owned installations (their conductors and equipment up to the service point), nor automotive vehicles, ships, aircraft, or underground mines — those have their own standards.

**NEC 90.3 — Code Arrangement.** Already covered in §2 of this module.

**NEC 90.4 — Enforcement.** The AHJ has authority to grant special permission, interpret rules, and approve equipment or methods. State or local amendments can modify the NEC, and those amendments are enforceable in that jurisdiction. The AHJ's word is final on local interpretation.

**NEC 90.5 — Mandatory Rules, Permissive Rules, and Explanatory Material.** Also covered in §2.

## Article 100 — Definitions

Article 100 is the dictionary. The apprentice exam loves Article 100 questions because they look easy but they're not — the code's definitions are often narrower or broader than common usage.

A short list of definitions every apprentice should be able to recite:

- **Accessible (as applied to equipment)** — admitting close approach; not guarded by locked doors, elevation, or other effective means.
- **Accessible (as applied to wiring methods)** — capable of being removed or exposed without damaging the building structure or finish.
- **Approved** — acceptable to the AHJ. (Not the same as "listed.")
- **Listed** — equipment or materials included in a list published by a qualified testing organization (like UL).
- **Labeled** — bearing a label of a qualified testing organization.
- **Identified (as applied to equipment)** — recognizable as suitable for the specific purpose or use.
- **Qualified Person** — has skills and knowledge related to the construction and operation of the equipment and the hazards involved.
- **Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ)** — the organization, office, or individual responsible for enforcing the code.

Definitions of voltage, current, ampacity, branch circuit, feeder, service, and continuous load also live here — review them before the exam.

## Article 110 — General Requirements for Electrical Installations

Article 110 covers things that apply to almost every installation.

**NEC 110.3 — Examination, Identification, Installation, Use, and Listing.** Equipment must be examined for suitability, mechanical strength, electrical insulation, wire-bending space, and so on. Equipment that is *listed and labeled* must be installed per any instructions included in the listing.

**NEC 110.14 — Electrical Connections.** Covers terminations, splices, and the all-important temperature rating issue. The temperature rating of the terminal limits how you read the ampacity table.

**NEC 110.16 — Arc-Flash Hazard Warning.** Electrical equipment likely to require examination while energized must be field-marked with an arc-flash warning.

**NEC 110.21, 110.22, 110.24** — markings, identification of disconnecting means, and available fault current. Required information that has to be on the equipment for it to be code-compliant.

**NEC 110.26 — Spaces About Electrical Equipment.** Working space — depth, width, headroom, illumination, dedicated equipment space. The dimensions vary with voltage and the conditions around the equipment, and this is one of the most-cited sections on inspections.$vp$, 6, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$All article references verified in NEC 2026 PDF: 90.1, 90.2, 90.3, 90.4, 90.5; 100 definitions; 110.3, 110.14, 110.16, 110.21, 110.22, 110.24, 110.26. Definitions paraphrased from Article 100 without quoting specific table values. No tabular ampacity or working-space dimensions stated.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 2, 4, $vp$Worked Example — Finding the Rule$vp$, $vp$worked_example$vp$, $vp$The skill the exam actually tests isn't memorizing the code — it's finding things fast. Here's a worked example of how to think your way through a code-lookup question.

## The scenario

The inspector asks why you installed a 200-amp panel with only 30 inches of clear space in front of it. He says it's not enough. You're sure he's right — but you need to cite the section that says so. Where do you look, and what do you find?

## Step 1 — Identify the article

The question is about working space around electrical equipment. That's general installation. **Chapter 1 — General**, **Article 110 — General Requirements for Electrical Installations**.

## Step 2 — Find the section

Inside Article 110, working space is **NEC 110.26 — Spaces About Electrical Equipment**.

## Step 3 — Read the structure of the section

NEC 110.26 is divided into subsections:

- **(A) Working Space.** The space in front of equipment that needs examination, adjustment, servicing, or maintenance while energized.
- **(B) Clear Spaces.** Working space cannot be used for storage.
- **(C) Entrance to and Egress from Working Space.** Doors, paths in/out.
- **(D) Illumination.**
- **(E) Headroom.**
- **(F) Dedicated Equipment Space.**

You want the depth in front of the panel — that's **110.26(A)**, and specifically **110.26(A)(1) Depth of Working Space**.

## Step 4 — Read the rule

NEC 110.26(A)(1) sends you to **Table 110.26(A)(1)** for required working-space depth. The table is organized by **nominal voltage to ground** (in voltage ranges) and **Condition 1, 2, or 3** — the condition depends on what's on the opposite wall (insulating wall, grounded surface, exposed live parts, etc.).

For a residential or light commercial 200-amp service operating at 120/240V (which is nominal voltage to ground of 120V — well within the 0-150V row of the table), and Condition 1 (drywall opposite the panel, no exposed live parts), the table specifies a minimum working depth. The actual value should be confirmed against the table in the current code cycle, but it's significantly more than 30 inches.

## Step 5 — Cite

The inspector is right. The relevant citation is **NEC 110.26(A)(1) and Table 110.26(A)(1)**. The fix is to relocate the panel or increase the clear depth to meet the table value for the applicable voltage and condition.

## What this problem taught you

- **Start with the article, then the section, then the subsection.** Don't dive straight at the table.
- **Sections often delegate to tables.** Reading the parent section tells you which row of the table to use.
- **Voltage-to-ground and condition matter** — pulling the wrong row of the table gives the wrong answer even if you found the right section.
- **Cite by section number, not by description.** The inspector and the AHJ talk in section numbers; "the working space rule" isn't a citation.

This is the exact mental flow you want to develop. On the exam, every code-lookup question rewards going article → section → subsection → table.$vp$, 5, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$NEC 110.26 structure verified in PDF: subsections (A) Working Space, (B) Clear Spaces, (C) Entrance/Egress, (D) Illumination, (E) Headroom, (F) Dedicated Equipment Space. Table 110.26(A)(1) organized by voltage-to-ground and Condition 1/2/3. No specific table value stated in the example (intentional — verification pattern emphasizes process over memorization).$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 2, 5, $vp$Where Apprentices Get Tripped Up$vp$, $vp$common_pitfalls$vp$, $vp$The NEC is precise, which makes it brutal when you read it wrong. These are the mistakes that cost exam points.

## 1. Treating Informational Notes as enforceable

An **Informational Note** is explanatory text, not a rule. The 3% voltage-drop "limit" most apprentices think they have to design to is actually in an Informational Note — it's a recommendation, not law (NEC 90.5(C) explains the distinction). On the exam, if a question asks "is this required," check whether the citation is to the code text proper or to an IN. They are NOT the same.

**Exceptions are different.** Exceptions ARE enforceable. They modify the rule that comes right before them. Don't confuse the two.

## 2. Looking up "the answer" instead of the rule that produces the answer

The code book doesn't give you answers — it gives you rules. If a question asks "what's the maximum overcurrent device for a 12 AWG copper conductor," you don't look up "20 A" — you look up NEC 240.4(D) which gives you the rule that tells you 20 A. On a poorly-worded exam question, going for the answer first leads you astray; going for the rule first keeps you honest.

## 3. Missing that Chapters 5-7 modify Chapters 1-4

Per NEC 90.3, Chapters 5, 6, and 7 **supplement or modify** Chapters 1-4. That means if you're working in a hazardous location (Chapter 5), the general wiring methods in Chapter 3 might be overridden by stricter rules in Article 500-series. Same for healthcare (Article 517 modifies general grounding rules), pools (Article 680 adds requirements), emergency systems (Article 700). Always check whether your installation falls under a Chapter 5-7 article first, before relying solely on Chapter 3 or 4.

## 4. Assuming "listed" and "approved" mean the same thing

They don't. **Listed** means a recognized testing lab (UL, ETL, CSA, etc.) put the equipment on a published list — it's been evaluated to a standard. **Approved** means the AHJ accepted it for the specific installation. You can have a listed product that the AHJ doesn't approve for a particular use, or an unlisted product the AHJ approves under special permission (NEC 90.4). The exam will test the distinction.

## 5. Skipping Article 100 because "definitions are easy"

They're not. Article 100 definitions are written to be precise, not intuitive. The code's definition of **continuous load**, **accessible**, **identified**, and **qualified person** are narrower than common usage, and exam questions exploit the gap. Read Article 100 cover-to-cover at least once before sitting for the exam.

## 6. Ignoring local amendments

The NEC sets the floor. Your state or city can amend it — usually to make it stricter, occasionally to make it more permissive. If you work in Chicago, you live with Chicago's electrical code (which historically banned NM cable in many installations). If you're testing under a state-administered exam, the state's amendments may be on the exam. Check the candidate handbook for your jurisdiction.$vp$, 5, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$NEC 90.5(C) (Informational Notes not enforceable), 90.3 (Chapter arrangement), 90.4 (Enforcement / AHJ authority), Article 100 (definitions of approved, listed, labeled), 240.4(D) (small conductor OCPD rule referenced as example). No specific tabular values cited.$vp$),
