- Use Chapter 9 Tables to find conduit fill percentages and conductor properties
- Recognize when a wiring method is not permitted (wet locations, hazardous locations, physical damage)
- Determine the appropriate ampacity column (60°C, 75°C, or 90°C) for a given termination

This module has a higher concentration of specific code values than M1 or M2. When in doubt, **look it up in your code book** — don't rely on memory for table values during the exam.$vp$, 2, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$Concept-only overview; no specific code citation needed.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 3, 2, $vp$Cables, Raceways, and Conductors$vp$, $vp$concept$vp$, $vp$Three categories of wiring method dominate residential and light commercial work: **cables**, **raceways**, and the **conductors** that go in raceways. Knowing the family each method belongs to tells you which article covers it.

## Cables

A **cable** is an assembly of insulated conductors with an outer jacket — you install it as a unit.

- **NM (Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable, "Romex")** — Article 334. Most common residential branch-circuit cable. Two or three insulated conductors plus a bare grounding conductor, wrapped in a thermoplastic jacket. Cheap, fast to install, restricted to dry locations in most applications.
- **AC (Armored Cable, "BX")** — Article 320. Insulated conductors inside a flexible spiral metal armor. The armor itself, along with an internal bonding strip, serves as the equipment grounding path.
- **MC (Metal-Clad Cable)** — Article 330. Similar to AC at a glance, but uses a separate green grounding conductor rather than relying on the armor. Allowed in more applications than AC.
- **UF (Underground Feeder)** — Article 340. Looks like NM but rated for direct burial and wet locations.
- **SE/USE (Service-Entrance / Underground Service-Entrance)** — Article 338. Used for service drops and lateral runs.

## Raceways

A **raceway** is an enclosed channel that you pull individual conductors through. The raceway itself doesn't conduct (well, sometimes it does — metallic raceways can serve as equipment grounding conductors per Article 250).

- **EMT (Electrical Metallic Tubing)** — Article 358. Thin-wall steel tubing. The most common metallic raceway in commercial work.
- **RMC (Rigid Metal Conduit)** — Article 344. Thick-wall steel. Heavy, durable, allowed almost everywhere.
- **IMC (Intermediate Metal Conduit)** — Article 342. Thinner than RMC but thicker than EMT. Splits the difference.
- **PVC (Rigid Polyvinyl Chloride Conduit)** — Article 352. Plastic. Common in underground and wet-location installations.
- **FMC (Flexible Metal Conduit)** — Article 348. Bendable steel for short connections to motors, fixtures, equipment that might move.
- **LFMC / LFNC (Liquidtight Flexible Metal / Nonmetallic Conduit)** — Articles 350 / 356. Flex with a watertight jacket for wet/oily locations.

## Conductors

Inside a raceway, the **individual conductors** are governed by **Article 310** (general) and **Article 400** (flexible cords).

The most common insulation types you'll see on an apprentice exam:

- **THHN/THWN-2** — thermoplastic, heat- and moisture-resistant. The workhorse 90°C-rated conductor.
- **THW** — older 75°C rating.
- **XHHW-2** — cross-linked polyethylene, 90°C, wet-location rated.

The **ampacity** of a given conductor depends on its size (AWG or kcmil), insulation type (which determines temperature rating), and the conditions of installation. **NEC Table 310.16** is the primary ampacity table for conductors in raceway or cable, with not more than three current-carrying conductors. The table has three columns of ampacity — 60°C, 75°C, and 90°C — and you have to know which one to use.

## The temperature-rating gotcha

This is where apprentices get stung. Even if your conductor has 90°C insulation, you can't always use the 90°C column of Table 310.16. Per **NEC 110.14(C)**, the ampacity is also limited by the **temperature rating of the terminal** on the equipment at each end. For circuits rated 100 amps or less (or sized 14 AWG through 1 AWG), terminations are usually rated 60°C or 75°C — so you use the 60°C or 75°C column even with 90°C-rated wire. The 90°C column is only available for derating calculations (adjustment and correction factors), not the final ampacity for the load.$vp$, 6, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$Article numbers verified in NEC 2026 PDF: 320 (AC), 330 (MC), 334 (NM), 338 (SE/USE), 340 (UF), 342 (IMC), 344 (RMC), 348 (FMC), 350 (LFMC), 352 (PVC), 356 (LFNC), 358 (EMT). Article 310 (conductors), 400 (cords). Table 310.16 column structure (60/75/90°C) verified. NEC 110.14(C) terminal temperature rule verified. NO specific ampacity values stated.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 3, 3, $vp$Support, Securing, and Conduit Fill$vp$, $vp$code_reference$vp$, $vp$Three topics show up over and over on the apprentice exam in this area: cable and raceway support, conduit fill, and conductor properties from Chapter 9.

## Cable support — NM cable

**NEC 334.30** governs securing and supporting NM cable. The cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding a maximum spacing, and within a specified short distance of every box, cabinet, conduit body, or fitting where the cable enters. The specific maximums are in the section text — review them in your code book; they're commonly tested.

NM cable also has stapling rules where it runs across or through framing: it has to be protected from physical damage and pulled tight enough that it doesn't sag between supports.

## Raceway support — EMT

**NEC 358.30** governs EMT support. EMT has to be **securely fastened in place** at maximum spacing intervals along the run, and within a short distance of each box, fitting, or termination. There's an exception that allows a slightly longer fastening distance from terminations where structural members make tight fastening impractical.

Other raceway articles have parallel support sections — **344.30** for RMC, **342.30** for IMC, **352.30** for PVC. Each one has its own rules; they're not all identical. Look up the specific raceway article when you're installing it.

## Conduit fill — Chapter 9 Table 1

**Chapter 9, Table 1** is the master conduit fill rule. It gives the maximum percentage of the conduit's cross-sectional area that can be occupied by conductors. The percentages depend on how many conductors are in the raceway:

- **One conductor** — higher fill percentage allowed
- **Two conductors** — the most restrictive (lowest percentage)
- **Three or more conductors** — middle of the range

The reason two conductors is the *most* restrictive — counterintuitive at first — is the geometry: two round conductors don't pack efficiently into a round raceway, so the table accounts for the wasted space.

The actual percentages from Chapter 9 Table 1 should be confirmed in your code book. They're commonly tested and easy to get wrong from memory.

To calculate fill in practice, you use:

1. **Chapter 9 Table 5** — Cross-sectional area for conductors (square inches per conductor for each insulation type and AWG size).
2. **Chapter 9 Table 4** — Allowable conductor fill area for each raceway type and trade size, based on the percentages from Table 1.

You sum up the conductor areas (Table 5), and pick a raceway size whose allowable fill (Table 4) accommodates the sum.

## Equipment grounding conductors

Whether or not an EGC counts toward conduit fill depends on the situation, but a key principle is in **NEC 250.122** — the EGC is sized from a table based on the rating of the overcurrent device protecting the circuit, not the size of the ungrounded conductors directly. Bigger circuit, bigger EGC.

## Wet vs dry locations

**NEC 300.5** and **300.6** address underground and protected installations. **Article 100** defines a **wet location** (subject to saturation with water), **damp location** (intermediate), and **dry location**. The wiring method has to be rated for the location it's installed in — UF for direct burial, THWN/XHHW for raceways in wet locations, and so on.$vp$, 5, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 334.30 (NM support), 358.30 (EMT support), 344.30/342.30/352.30 (other raceway support), Chapter 9 Table 1 (conduit fill percentages), Table 4 (allowable fill area), Table 5 (conductor dimensions), 250.122 (EGC sizing), 300.5/300.6 (wet locations), Article 100 (location definitions). Specific tabular percentages and ampacity values intentionally NOT stated — flagged as look-up.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 3, 4, $vp$Worked Example — Choosing a Method and a Size$vp$, $vp$worked_example$vp$, $vp$Let's walk through a realistic decision: you need to run a circuit from a panel to a commercial breakroom that's 75 feet away through unfinished commercial ceiling space. The load is a 20-amp dedicated branch for an appliance.

## Step 1 — Pick a wiring method

The space is commercial and the run is exposed in the ceiling. NM cable is not the right call here — it's not typically allowed exposed in commercial buildings, and many jurisdictions restrict NM to dwellings entirely.

The conventional choice is **EMT (Article 358)** — thin-wall steel tubing, dry-location rated, suitable for commercial general-purpose runs. You'll pull individual conductors through it.

## Step 2 — Pick conductors

For a 20-amp branch circuit, **NEC 240.4(D)(5)** sets the overcurrent device maximum for 12 AWG copper at 20 A. So 12 AWG copper is your minimum size — assuming standard conditions and a 75°C terminal rating (typical for breakers up to 100 A per **NEC 110.14(C)**).

For wire type, **THWN-2** is the typical choice in EMT — 90°C insulation, rated for dry and wet locations, easy to pull. The 90°C rating gives you headroom if you ever need to derate (more than three current-carrying conductors in the raceway, for example).

## Step 3 — Plan the run

The conductor count for a typical 20-amp single-phase 120V branch circuit is:

- 1 ungrounded ("hot") conductor — 12 AWG THWN-2
- 1 grounded ("neutral") conductor — 12 AWG THWN-2
- 1 equipment grounding conductor — 12 AWG (per **NEC 250.122** for a 20-amp circuit)

Three conductors total. Two current-carrying (hot and neutral); the EGC is not current-carrying except during a fault.

## Step 4 — Conduit fill

You're pulling three 12 AWG THWN-2 conductors. To verify your EMT trade size:

1. Look up the cross-sectional area for 12 AWG THWN-2 in **Chapter 9 Table 5** — sum three conductor areas.
2. Look up the allowable fill area for a candidate EMT size (start with ½-inch trade size) in **Chapter 9 Table 4**.
3. Compare: if your conductor area total is less than or equal to the table's allowable fill at "Over 2 Wires" (3-or-more-conductor column, the 40% fill row from Table 1), the size works.

For three 12 AWG THWN-2 conductors, ½-inch EMT is generally sufficient — but **verify the exact values in your code book before committing**, because the fill margins are close enough that you don't want to eyeball it.

## Step 5 — Support the raceway

Per **NEC 358.30**, the EMT must be:

- Securely fastened at the maximum interval the section specifies (look up the exact distance — it's commonly tested)
- Within the section's specified short distance of every termination

Apply the support rule from one end of the run to the other.

## Step 6 — Final check

- Cable type appropriate for location? ✓ (EMT, dry, commercial)
- OCPD properly sized for conductor? ✓ (20 A on 12 AWG per 240.4(D)(5))
- Terminal temperature rule satisfied? ✓ (75°C column for 100A-or-less device, 12 AWG = 25 A in 75°C col which is well above 20 A)
- Conduit fill within limits? ✓ (verified in Chapter 9)
- Support per article requirements? ✓ (per 358.30)
- EGC sized correctly? ✓ (12 AWG per 250.122 for a 20-amp circuit)

## What this problem taught you

- Picking a wiring method is a decision based on location, occupancy, and physical conditions — not just whatever's cheapest.
- Conductor sizing is a multi-step decision: OCPD rating, conductor ampacity from Table 310.16, terminal temperature rating from 110.14(C), and adjustment factors if any apply.
- Conduit fill is a table-driven calculation, not a guess. Use Tables 1, 4, and 5 together.
- The EGC has its own sizing rule (250.122) separate from the ungrounded conductors.$vp$, 7, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 240.4(D)(5) (12 AWG → 20 A OCPD, verified prior session), 110.14(C) (terminal temperature), Article 358 (EMT), 358.30 (EMT support), 250.122 (EGC sizing), Chapter 9 Tables 1/4/5 (conduit fill). EGC size of 12 AWG for 20 A circuit is verified from Table 250.122 (standard value). 75°C ampacity of 12 AWG (25 A) is verified standard from Table 310.16. SME-flagged for table-value verification and to confirm EMT fill arithmetic at ½-inch.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 3, 5, $vp$Where Apprentices Get Tripped Up$vp$, $vp$common_pitfalls$vp$, $vp$Wiring methods is fertile ground for exam mistakes. The rules are precise, the abbreviations look alike, and the tables are easy to misread.

## 1. Using the wrong ampacity column

The single most common error: assuming THHN at 90°C lets you load the wire to the 90°C column of Table 310.16. **NEC 110.14(C)** caps you at the terminal temperature. For most equipment rated 100 A or less, terminations are 60°C or 75°C — even if both ends of the wire are 90°C-rated. The 90°C column is for derating math (adjustment and correction), not the final ampacity for the load.

## 2. Confusing AC and MC cable

They look almost identical from the outside — both have flexible metal armor. The difference: AC cable (Article 320) uses the armor + an internal bonding strip as the EGC. MC cable (Article 330) has a separate insulated EGC, and the armor is not the grounding path. The result is that MC is allowed in more places than AC. Always check the cable marking and the article that applies.

## 3. Reading Chapter 9 Table 1 wrong

Table 1 gives the maximum fill percentage based on **number of conductors**. The trap: **two conductors is the lowest percentage**, not three. Apprentices read it left-to-right and assume "more conductors, lower percentage" — but two-conductor fill is the most restrictive because of how two round conductors stack inefficiently in a round pipe. Then it goes UP again at three-or-more.

## 4. Forgetting that the EGC sometimes counts toward fill

Even though the EGC isn't a current-carrying conductor for ampacity adjustment purposes, it still takes up space inside the raceway. When you do a fill calculation, you include the EGC in the conductor count and area total. Skipping it gets you a raceway that fits on paper but is overstuffed in real life.

## 5. Choosing the wrong cable type for the location

NM cable in a wet location, AC cable in a hazardous classified location, EMT direct-buried — these are all method/location mismatches that fail inspection. The "Uses Permitted" section at the start of each Chapter 3 article lists where the method is allowed; the "Uses Not Permitted" section lists where it isn't. Read both.

## 6. Treating the support rules as one-size-fits-all

NM cable support spacing is different from EMT support spacing, which is different from RMC, which is different from PVC. Each article has its own .30 section (or similar) with the rules. There's no universal "support every X feet" rule that works across all methods. When in doubt, flip to the article for the method you're installing.

## 7. Misapplying 240.4(D) for "small conductors"

**NEC 240.4(D)** sets the maximum overcurrent device for 14, 12, and 10 AWG conductors regardless of what Table 310.16 says their ampacity is. This is a separate rule from the ampacity table. Even if the 90°C column lists 12 AWG at 30 A, you cannot put a 30-amp breaker on it — 240.4(D)(5) caps you at 20 A. The "small conductor rule" overrides the ampacity table in this size range.$vp$, 5, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 110.14(C) (terminal temp), Article 320 vs 330 (AC vs MC), Chapter 9 Table 1 (fill percentages — two-conductor row is the most restrictive, verified), 240.4(D) (small conductor rule, verified in PDF prior session). Pitfalls are common knowledge in the field; rules cited rather than specific table values quoted.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 3, 6, $vp$Test Yourself$vp$, $vp$self_check$vp$, $vp$Five reflection questions. Work through them in your head — and if a code reference comes to mind, flip to it and verify you've got the right one.

1. You're pulling four 12 AWG THWN-2 conductors through ½-inch EMT. Which Chapter 9 tables do you consult, and in what order, to verify the fill is within code?

2. A 90°C-rated THHN conductor is installed on equipment with 75°C terminals. Which ampacity column of Table 310.16 governs the load on the wire — and which column is used for any adjustment or correction math?

3. AC cable and MC cable look almost identical. What's the practical difference between them, and which article covers each?

4. Why is the maximum fill percentage for two conductors *lower* than the percentage for three or more conductors in the same raceway?

5. For a 20-amp branch circuit, what's the minimum equipment grounding conductor size in copper, and which section of the NEC tells you?

