    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 4, 1, $vp$What This Module Covers$vp$, $vp$overview$vp$, $vp$Residential wiring is what most apprentices learn first and what most exam questions test heavily. The rules are scattered across several NEC articles, but they cluster around a few themes: how power gets from the utility to the panel (service entrance), how branch circuits are sized and protected, how receptacles get laid out in living spaces, and what kind of fault protection (GFCI, AFCI) is required where.

NEC 2026 brought significant changes to the residential rules — most notably the addition of the 10-amp branch circuit rating, expanded AFCI requirements, and a broader scope for GFCI protection at dwellings. If you trained on an older code cycle, some of what you "know" needs to be unlearned.

**By the end of this module, you'll be able to:**

- Describe the service entrance components from the utility connection to the main panel
- Identify the minimum service size required for a dwelling and the load calculation that justifies it
- Apply branch circuit sizing rules including the new 10-amp option in NEC 2026
- Place receptacles in living spaces, kitchens, and bathrooms per code spacing rules
- Determine where GFCI and AFCI protection is required in dwelling units
- Recognize the limitations on 10-amp circuits — what they can and can't serve

This module is heavy on specific values from the code. **Bring your code book**, and flag any value you're not 100% sure on for SME review.$vp$, 2, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, false, false, $vp$Concept-only overview; flags NEC 2026 changes as a category.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 4, 2, $vp$From the Utility to the Outlet$vp$, $vp$concept$vp$, $vp$A typical single-family home gets electrical service through a chain of components, each governed by its own NEC article. Understanding the chain makes the code rules make sense.

## The service entrance

Power arrives from the utility — either overhead (a service drop from a pole) or underground (a service lateral from a transformer or pedestal). The point where utility ownership ends and customer ownership begins is the **service point** (defined in Article 100). Everything from the service point to the main disconnect is the **service entrance**, governed by **Article 230**.

The components, in order:
- **Service drop or lateral** (utility-owned)
- **Service point** (the boundary)
- **Service-entrance conductors** — usually SE cable (Article 338) or conductors in raceway, sized to carry the full service load
- **Service equipment** — meter base, main disconnect, and main overcurrent protection
- **Grounding electrode system** (Article 250) — tied in at the service

## Service size

**NEC 230.79(C)** sets the minimum service size for a one-family dwelling at **100 amperes**. In practice, most modern homes use 200-amp services because the calculated load with central AC, an electric range, electric dryer, water heater, and EVSE quickly exceeds 100 A.

The actual service size required is set by a **load calculation** — Article 120 (which used to be Article 220 before NEC 2026 reorganized it). There's a "standard method" and an "optional method" (the optional method in **NEC 120.82** is simpler for typical dwellings). Either way, you sum up loads with demand factors and pick a service rated for the result.

## The main panel

The main panel (or "loadcenter" in residential lingo) houses the main disconnect and the branch-circuit breakers. From here, branch circuits run out to the loads.

## Branch circuits

A **branch circuit** is the wiring between the final overcurrent device and the outlet. **Article 210** governs branch circuits. Standard branch-circuit ratings per **NEC 210.18** are:

**10, 15, 20, 30, 40, and 50 amperes.**

The **10-amp rating is new in NEC 2026**. It's allowed for fixed loads — hardwired lighting, smoke detectors, and similar — but per **NEC 210.18 Exception No. 2**, a 10-amp branch circuit **shall not supply receptacle outlets**. If you want a receptacle, you need at least a 15-amp circuit.

## Receptacle layout

Receptacle placement is one of the most-tested topics on the apprentice exam. The big rules:

- **NEC 210.52(A)(1)** — in habitable rooms, receptacles are placed so no point along the floor line of any wall is more than **6 ft (1.8 m)** from a receptacle. The intent is that anything plugged in with a 6-foot cord can reach an outlet without crossing a doorway.
- **NEC 210.52(C)(1)** — in kitchens, countertop receptacles are placed so no point along the wall counter is more than **24 in. (600 mm)** from a receptacle. This drives the typical "outlet every 4 feet" along a counter run.

Bathrooms, hallways, outdoor spaces, garages, basements, and laundry areas each have their own subsection of **210.52**. Each one specifies how many receptacles, where they go, and whether they require GFCI or AFCI protection.$vp$, 6, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 230.79(C) (100 A dwelling minimum, verified prior session), Article 120 / 120.82 (load calc, NEC 2026 renumber from 220 — verified), 210.18 (branch circuit ratings 10/15/20/30/40/50 A, verified), 210.18 Exception No. 2 (10A no receptacles, verified), 210.52(A)(1) (6 ft rule, verified), 210.52(C)(1) (24 in. kitchen counter rule, verified). Article 100 'service point', Article 230 (service entrance), Article 338 (SE cable), Article 250 (grounding electrode), Article 210 (branch circuits) — all verified.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 4, 3, $vp$GFCI, AFCI, and the NEC 2026 Changes$vp$, $vp$code_reference$vp$, $vp$Fault protection is where NEC 2026 made the biggest residential changes. Old rules of thumb may be wrong. Look these up fresh.

## GFCI protection — NEC 210.8

**Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI)** protection trips when current leaks to ground — protecting people from electric shock. **NEC 210.8(A)** covers dwelling units; **210.8(B)** covers other-than-dwelling locations.

For dwellings, **210.8(A)** lists locations where GFCI protection is required for 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles. The list has expanded significantly across recent code cycles. In NEC 2026 it includes:

- Bathrooms
- Garages and accessory buildings
- Outdoors
- Crawl spaces and unfinished basements
- Kitchens (counter receptacles and others within a defined distance of the sink)
- Sinks and dishwashers
- Boathouses, bathtubs, and shower stalls
- Laundry areas
- Indoor damp and wet locations
- Other specific locations enumerated in the section

NEC 2026 specifically added or expanded several of these — including a broader rule on **outdoor outlets at dwellings** in **210.8(F)** and **crawl-space lighting outlets at 120 V or less** in **210.8(C)**. Read the current section text — the room-by-room list isn't optional knowledge.

## AFCI protection — NEC 210.12

**Arc-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (AFCI)** protection trips when it detects the electrical signature of a series or parallel arcing fault — protecting against fires from damaged wiring.

In NEC 2026, **210.12(B)** requires AFCI protection on all **10-, 15-, and 20-ampere** branch circuits supplying outlets or devices installed in 14 specified types of dwelling rooms or areas. The list includes: kitchens, family rooms, dining rooms, living rooms, parlors, libraries, dens, bedrooms, sunrooms, recreation rooms, closets, hallways, laundry areas, and similar areas.

Note the **10-ampere inclusion is new in 2026** — because 10-amp circuits themselves are new in 2026. If you're running a hardwired 10-amp circuit for fixed lighting in a bedroom, it needs AFCI protection just like a 15- or 20-amp circuit does.

## How GFCI and AFCI interact

GFCI and AFCI are different protections solving different problems — shock vs. fire — and many circuits require both. **Dual-function (combination GFCI/AFCI) devices** are listed for circuits that need both protections, or the protections can be provided separately at the breaker and the receptacle.

## The 10-amp circuit limits — NEC 210.18 Exception No. 2

The 10-amp circuit added in NEC 2026 has one critical limitation: **it shall not supply receptacle outlets**. It's restricted to hardwired loads — fixed luminaires, hardwired smoke and CO alarms, and similar. The rationale is that a homeowner can't plug a vacuum cleaner into a 10-amp circuit and trip it on the inrush; the 10-amp rating is too low for general-purpose use.

If a designer wants to save copper by running smaller wire on hardwired loads, the 10-amp branch is now an option. For anything with receptacles, the minimum stays at 15 amps.

## Required circuits in dwellings — small appliance and laundry

**NEC 210.11(C)(1)** requires at least **two 20-amp small-appliance branch circuits** for receptacle outlets in the kitchen, pantry, breakfast room, dining room, or similar areas — and these circuits cannot supply other outlets.

**NEC 210.11(C)(2)** requires at least **one 20-amp branch circuit** for laundry receptacles, and that circuit cannot supply anything else.

**NEC 210.11(C)(3)** requires at least one **20-amp branch circuit** for bathroom receptacles. It can either be dedicated to one bathroom (and supply other outlets in that bathroom) or supply only receptacles across multiple bathrooms.$vp$, 6, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 210.8 (GFCI requirements, verified in PDF including 2026 expansions), 210.8(A) (dwelling locations list, verified — 16+ items per prior session), 210.8(C) (crawl space lighting GFCI, NEC 2026 added — verified), 210.8(F) (all outdoor dwelling outlets, NEC 2026 added — verified), 210.12(B) (AFCI on 10/15/20A in 14 dwelling room types, verified), 210.18 Exception No. 2 (10A no receptacles, verified), 210.11(C)(1)(2)(3) (small appliance, laundry, bathroom dedicated circuits — verified for 20A and 'cannot supply other outlets' rule). NEC 2026 changes specifically flagged.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 4, 4, $vp$Worked Example — Wiring a New Kitchen$vp$, $vp$worked_example$vp$, $vp$A homeowner is remodeling a kitchen. They want code-compliant wiring including all required circuits, proper receptacle layout, and the correct fault protections. Walk through the design.

## The space

- Standard rectangular kitchen, 12 ft × 14 ft
- L-shaped counter run with a sink in the middle
- Range, refrigerator, dishwasher, and microwave
- Existing service is 200 A, capacity for new circuits available

## Step 1 — Required dedicated circuits

Per **NEC 210.11(C)(1)**, kitchen countertop receptacles must be served by **at least two 20-amp small-appliance branch circuits**. These circuits cannot supply other outlets (no lighting, no other rooms).

The range will be on its own dedicated circuit — typically 40-amp or 50-amp 240V (NEC 220.55 / 120.55 dwellings demand). The dishwasher and disposal each generally get their own 20-amp circuit. Refrigerator on either one of the small-appliance circuits or a dedicated 20-amp circuit (the code allows either, but a dedicated fridge circuit is best practice and required in some jurisdictions).

## Step 2 — Countertop receptacle layout

Per **NEC 210.52(C)(1)**, every point along the wall counter (12 in. or wider) must be within **24 in. (600 mm)** of a receptacle. For our L-shaped counter, that means measuring along the counter from each corner and each end and placing receptacles so no 24-inch gap exists.

The sink interrupts the counter — per the section text, the counter on each side of the sink is measured separately, with the same 24-inch rule applying on each side.

**Island and peninsula counters** have their own subsection (**210.52(C)(2)**) — at least one receptacle is required for each, with specific rules on placement.

## Step 3 — GFCI protection

Per **NEC 210.8(A)**, all 125V through 250V receptacles in the kitchen counter area require **GFCI protection**. This applies to the small-appliance circuit receptacles, the dishwasher receptacle, and any receptacle within the kitchen sink proximity.

GFCI can be provided at the breaker (whole circuit) or at the receptacle (load-side outlets downstream protected too). Common practice is a GFCI breaker for the small-appliance circuits since both are required.

## Step 4 — AFCI protection

Per **NEC 210.12(B)**, AFCI is required on 15- and 20-amp branch circuits in kitchens (kitchen is on the dwelling-room list). The 20-amp small-appliance circuits therefore need AFCI too — and since they also need GFCI, a **dual-function GFCI/AFCI breaker** is the cleanest solution. Two of them, one for each small-appliance circuit.

## Step 5 — Wire size and breaker

Each 20-amp circuit uses 12 AWG copper conductors (per **NEC 240.4(D)(5)** small conductor rule — 12 AWG → 20 A OCPD maximum). The breakers are 20-amp dual-function (GFCI/AFCI).

The range circuit uses larger conductor sized per the range nameplate and the demand factors in **120.55** (formerly 220.55 in NEC 2023 and earlier).

## Step 6 — Cable type and routing

For residential wiring inside the dwelling, **NM cable (Article 334)** is typical for general-purpose branch circuits. Support per **334.30**: securing every 4½ ft maximum and within 12 in. of every box. Cable enters boxes through listed clamps or connectors.

## Final summary of circuits

- Two 20-amp small-appliance circuits (12 AWG NM, dual-function GFCI/AFCI)
- One 20-amp dishwasher circuit (or shared with disposal per local practice, GFCI required, AFCI required)
- One 20-amp disposal circuit (if separate)
- One 240V range circuit, sized per nameplate and 120.55 demand
- One 20-amp refrigerator circuit if dedicated (best practice)

## What this problem taught you

- Kitchen wiring isn't one circuit — it's a *minimum of two* small-appliance circuits plus dedicated circuits for major appliances.
- GFCI and AFCI are independently required and often both apply to the same circuit. Dual-function breakers solve both with one device.
- Receptacle layout has a specific measurement rule (24 in. on counters, 6 ft elsewhere) — design to the rule, not by feel.
- The same 12 AWG conductor and 20-amp breaker keep appearing because of the small-conductor rule in 240.4(D)(5).$vp$, 7, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 210.11(C)(1) (two small-appliance circuits, verified), 210.52(C)(1) (24 in. counter rule, verified), 210.52(C)(2) (island/peninsula receptacles, verified in PDF), 210.8(A) (GFCI in kitchen, verified), 210.12(B) (AFCI in kitchens, verified), 240.4(D)(5) (12 AWG/20A, verified prior session), 334.30 (NM support 4.5 ft / 12 in., verified prior session), Article 334 (NM cable), 120.55 (formerly 220.55 — range demand, NEC 2026 renumber). Range OCPD typical sizes (40-50 A) stated as 'typical' rather than specific value. Dishwasher/disposal dedicated-circuit guidance noted as best practice rather than strict code requirement.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 4, 5, $vp$Where Apprentices Get Tripped Up$vp$, $vp$common_pitfalls$vp$, $vp$Residential code rules look simple but the details bite. These are the errors that show up over and over.

## 1. Putting a receptacle on a 10-amp circuit

Brand new in NEC 2026: the 10-amp branch circuit exists for hardwired loads only. **NEC 210.18 Exception No. 2** explicitly bars 10-amp circuits from supplying receptacle outlets. If a question shows a 10-amp circuit feeding a receptacle, that's a code violation — period.

## 2. Forgetting that small-appliance circuits can't supply other outlets

**NEC 210.11(C)(1)** requires at least two 20-amp small-appliance branch circuits for the kitchen, pantry, breakfast room, and dining room — and these circuits **shall have no other outlets**. Apprentices sometimes wire a kitchen ceiling light off a small-appliance circuit because there's spare capacity. That's a violation. Lighting needs its own circuit.

## 3. Misreading the 6-foot habitable-room rule

**NEC 210.52(A)(1)** says no point along the **floor line** of any wall is more than 6 ft from a receptacle — and the "floor line" measurement runs along the wall, not in a straight line across the room. A long wall with a single receptacle in the middle of it is non-compliant if either end is more than 6 ft from the receptacle, even though a 6-foot cord could reach. Measure along the wall.

Wall sections at least 2 ft wide (e.g., short walls between doorways) count for the receptacle requirement; sections shorter than 2 ft do not. Floor-to-ceiling windows and similar features can also affect what counts as "wall space."

## 4. Assuming AFCI is "only for bedrooms"

That was true in old code cycles. It hasn't been true for years, and certainly isn't true in NEC 2026. **NEC 210.12(B)** lists 14 types of rooms or areas where AFCI is required on 10/15/20-amp dwelling branch circuits. The list includes kitchens, family rooms, hallways, closets, and a lot of other areas apprentices don't expect. Read the current list.

## 5. Skipping GFCI on the dishwasher circuit

A hardwired dishwasher used to be exempt from GFCI protection. **NEC 210.8(D)** now requires GFCI protection for specific appliances (including dishwashers) at dwellings, hardwired or cord-connected. Check the current section text for the full list — it has grown across recent cycles.

## 6. Picking the wrong service size by skipping the load calc

NEC 230.79(C) sets the **minimum** dwelling service at 100 A, but for any modern home with central AC, electric appliances, and EVSE charging, the calculated load almost always exceeds 100 A. You can't pick a service size by gut feel — run the load calc per Article 120 (standard method) or 120.82 (optional method for dwellings) and size to the result.

## 7. Treating the optional method as a shortcut

**NEC 120.82** (optional dwelling method) is simpler than the standard method, but it has its own rules — including specific demand factors and a minimum service size. It's not "the easy way" — it's a different method with its own discipline. Don't mix and match terms from the standard method into the optional method or vice versa.$vp$, 5, $vp$opus_2026_05_24$vp$, true, false, $vp$Sourced from NEC 210.18 Exception No. 2 (10A no receptacles), 210.11(C)(1) (small-appliance circuit dedication), 210.52(A)(1) (6 ft habitable rule + 2 ft wall section qualifier — 2 ft threshold verified in PDF), 210.12(B) (AFCI 14-room list), 210.8(D) (specific appliance GFCI — verified in NEC 2026), 230.79(C) (100 A minimum), Article 120 / 120.82 (load calc methods). All NEC 2026 changes flagged for SME confirmation.$vp$),
    ($vp$APPRENTICE$vp$, 4, 6, $vp$Test Yourself$vp$, $vp$self_check$vp$, $vp$Five questions. Take your time on these — residential wiring is where most apprentice-exam questions live, and a strong grasp here pays off heavily.

1. Per NEC 2026, what is the new minimum branch-circuit ampere rating, and what one major load type is it prohibited from supplying?

2. A dwelling's kitchen needs how many minimum dedicated small-appliance branch circuits, at what ampere rating, and what kind of loads can those circuits NOT supply?

3. A receptacle in a habitable room must be placed so no point along the wall floor line exceeds what distance from a receptacle? Does that distance measure along the wall or across the room?

4. A 20-amp branch circuit feeds receptacles in a kitchen. What two types of fault protection does NEC 2026 require on this circuit, and what single device type provides both?

5. NEC 2026 renumbered the load-calculation article from 220 to what number? Where does the "optional method" for dwellings live within it?

